-- L1_exchange.hm
-- Layer 1: Exchange without money
-- hyperoptimization | knowledge, understanding, deals and entangled decisions
--
-- Imports: L0_viability (the game, the kernel, the greatest sustaining law, the quorum map and the
--          bit as the only unit of price). Nothing else; no term of hyperethics, hyperlogic or
--          ordinatics is imported.
--
-- USER-DECLARED (Tyler Roost, 2026-10-11), the starting point of this layer, verbatim:
--   "subagents should always be haiku 5.5 just so you know, also for there to be no money we need
--    information asymmetric deal making through informationally richer channels than single
--    dimensional $. Also for a decision to be makable in the first place a comprehensive
--    understanding of the material and its impacts is a necessity, this sets the quorum size, and
--    also determines the barrier to entry for decision making, for deal making we should utilize
--    quantum game theoretical optionality to determine entangled decisions which themselves by
--    simply existing in an entangled state provide value for exclusively win win deals since we no
--    longer use money and instead represent deals by value of freedom allowed and degree to which
--    those freedoms allow other freedoms and the dimensions along which those freedoms all cohere
--    with the overall systems lawful existence."
-- Quoted exactly, spelling and spacing included. The first clause is a standing instruction about how
-- the work is done (every subagent uses the smallest model tier); it is not part of the model and is recorded in
-- PROVENANCE.md and in the handoff. Tyler's statements are authoritative for his own aims; they do not
-- by themselves verify an external claim, and this file does not treat them as doing so.
--
-- USER-DECLARED (Tyler Roost, 2026-10-11, later the same day, after the exchange layer had been
-- reported), verbatim:
--   "There is no possible uniform start, the world currently has money, we need a way to extrapolate
--    from current status games into a future with no money, the only utopic futures are ones with no
--    money."
-- Quoted exactly. It withdrew the candidate this layer had named for the starting allocation, a
-- uniformly random one, and set the task of STANDING below. "The only utopic futures are ones with no
-- money" is the owner's claim about what is good; this file neither derives nor tests it.
--
-- WHAT THIS LAYER IS. L0 asks what law a system that wants to exist is under. L1 asks how parties who
-- see only parts of the world make deals about that law without a number to settle them. Six answers,
-- each a finite model with an exact oracle.
--   KNOWLEDGE. What a party cannot see it must treat as unsafe, so what it can certify as permitted is a
--   part of the greatest law and grows as it learns. What is exchanged is a certificate, a set of
--   situations that supports itself, and not a price: a number cannot be checked for safety.
--   UNDERSTANDING. A decision is makeable only if the people deciding between them understand what it
--   rests on and what rests on it. That sets the quorum (the cover, and the attendance at which whoever
--   comes understands) and the barrier (the share of the footprint a person must understand), and the
--   barrier is paid for in capture resistance.
--   DEALS. An opening of the law is valued by a vector: the freedom it allows, the freedom that enables,
--   and the dimensions along which it coheres with lawful existence. Coherence is a constraint and not a
--   currency; within lawful existence nothing competes; where options compete a price cannot see the
--   balanced one.
--   ENTANGLEMENT. Parties who cannot talk and win or lose together do better with a shared entangled
--   state than any classical strategy can, by exact amounts, and the state carries no message.
--   RIVAL FREEDOMS. A freedom that one party holds at a time cannot be given to another for nothing, so
--   win-win needs a rule. For indivisible goods, one to each party, with strict private rankings, top
--   trading cycles exchanges them with no money so that nobody ends below where they began, no group can
--   do better among itself, and no party gains by misreporting; each party can audit the outcome from its
--   own ranking and a public record; and what each party ends with is decided by the starting allocation,
--   which nothing here derives.
--   STANDING. Whatever the start is, an exchange by rankings alone can move only the loops of envy (an
--   agent envies another whose good it ranks above its own); what is left is a ladder, a pecking order in
--   which each holds its favourite of what those before it left. A ladder made by wealth is frozen by
--   abolishing money while keeping holdings, and what thaws a ladder is a difference in the direction of
--   what agents value, not in its strength.
--
-- WHAT IT IS NOT. Win-win holds by construction here, because the permissions are not rival and a
-- gain is a permission nobody could certify (nd-win-win-by-construction). Understanding is a set
-- handed in; nothing measures it (nd-understanding-is-measured). That human or institutional decisions
-- can be entangled is a proposal with no witness (nd-institutions-are-entangled). Games whose parties
-- have opposed interests are not modelled (nd-quantum-dilemma). Rival freedoms are modelled only as
-- indivisible goods held one to a party under strict private rankings (ax-rival-is-indivisible); ties,
-- several units and tastes that depend on others are not (nd-rival-freedoms), and who holds what at the
-- start is given and not derived (nd-endowment). Real status is a taste that depends on what others hold
-- (nd-status-is-envy), and how to unfreeze a ladder other than by a change in tastes is not modelled
-- (nd-ladder-dissolution).
--
-- Layer boundary: nothing here imports another package. Checks that need a record of the family read a
-- golden file hyperstratum produced.
--
-- Status: FORM (the finite-game theorems of Section IV, checked exhaustively on random small games, by
--               independent oracles, or in exact rationals);
--         FRAME (the readings: ignorance as pessimism, understanding as a set, an opening as a vector,
--               an entangled decision as measurements on a shared state);
--         HYPER / OPEN residuals documented, NOT discharged: Section V.
--
-- Checked status: every derive in this file is executable against finite structures by the test suite
-- and, with its evidence, by `python -m hyperoptimization decisions`. A passing check CONFIRMS nothing
-- universally; one counterexample REFUTES.
-- =============================================================================


-- =============================================================================
-- SECTION I: PRIMITIVES
-- =============================================================================

primitive Participant :: Type
    -- Something that can see part of the world and take part in a decision or a deal.

primitive Knowledge :: Type
    -- The set of states (or nodes of a graph of reliance) a participant can see, or understands.

primitive Opening :: Type
    -- A pair of laws, one permitting no more than the other: a deal about what is permitted.

primitive Dimension :: Type
    -- A kind of freedom along which permissions are counted (by default the action).


-- =============================================================================
-- SECTION II: OPAQUE STRUCTURE
-- =============================================================================

opaque sees :: Participant -> State -> Norm
    -- Whether a participant can see a state, so that it may reason about what happens there.

opaque acts_at :: Participant -> State -> Norm
    -- Whether a participant is the one who acts at a state: its territory.

opaque relies_on :: State -> State -> Norm
    -- Whether one state's future depends on another's: the graph in which a footprint is read.


-- =============================================================================
-- SECTION III: ADOPTED AXIOMS
-- Each is an assumption of the model, adopted and not derived.
-- =============================================================================

axiom ax-ignorance-is-pessimism:
    -- What a participant cannot see it treats as unsafe, because an unknown state may be the one that
    -- ends everything. Its view of the game is the same game with the safe set cut down to what it sees.
    -- Adopted: it is the weakest assumption that makes a certificate sound.

axiom ax-understanding-is-a-set:
    -- Each participant understands a set of nodes, given and not measured. Whether and how that could be
    -- measured is not modelled (nd-understanding-is-measured).

axiom ax-common-interest:
    -- In a game of deals everyone wins or loses together, so a deal is exclusively win-win by definition
    -- of the game. Games with opposed interests are not modelled (nd-quantum-dilemma).

axiom ax-freedom-is-non-rival:
    -- A permission granted to one party leaves every other party's permissions as they were, and
    -- knowledge given away leaves the giver's view unchanged. Without this, win-win is not automatic
    -- (ax-rival-is-indivisible, nd-rival-freedoms).

axiom ax-rival-is-indivisible:
    -- A rival freedom (a slot, a seat, a unit that runs out) is an indivisible good that one party holds
    -- at a time. Every party holds one and ranks all of them strictly, in private. Adopted: it is the
    -- model in which an exchange without money has known guarantees. Ties, several units and tastes that
    -- depend on who else gets what are outside it (nd-rival-freedoms).

axiom ax-standing-is-envy:
    -- A party's standing, in an exchange by rankings alone, is read from whom it envies: it envies another
    -- when it ranks that party's good above its own. A status game is a common ranking, everyone wanting
    -- the same good. Adopted: it is the reading under which what an exchange can move from a start can be
    -- said exactly. Tastes that depend on who else holds what are outside it (nd-status-is-envy).

axiom ax-wealth-picks-first:
    -- Holdings made by money are read as a start made by letting the wealthiest party pick first, then
    -- the next wealthiest, and so on. Adopted: it is the most that "the wealthy choose first" can mean in
    -- a model with rankings and no prices. A market is not a serial dictatorship.

axiom ax-the-dial-is-a-straight-mix:
    -- Between the ranking a common price induces and a party's own, the party scores a good by the
    -- weights (1 - t) * price + t * its own. Adopted: one path among many, the one on which a ranking of
    -- two goods can change at most once, which makes the dial exactly computable.


-- =============================================================================
-- SECTION IV: DERIVES
-- Each is executable: the test named in DESIGN.md's spec map checks it.
-- =============================================================================

derive d-view-is-sound:
    -- What a view certifies lies inside the kernel and inside the greatest law; knowing more never
    -- certifies less; a view closed under the moves it can make certifies exactly what is in the kernel
    -- on the states it holds.
    -- Status: FORM (every subset of every sampled game, with and without corrigibility).

derive d-certificate-is-a-self-supporting-set:
    -- A state is certified by a view exactly when the view holds a set that supports itself around it
    -- (each state in it has an allowed move that sends every nature choice back into it). The smallest
    -- knowledge a permission needs is therefore a smallest self-supporting set.
    -- Status: FORM (every subset of every sampled game).

derive d-pooling-needs-a-border:
    -- Pooling the views of parties that each hold a territory certifies at least the union of what each
    -- did, and sometimes strictly more; territories that no allowed move crosses gain nothing, and every
    -- gain sampled had a crossing. Two hamlets that shelter in each other in a storm need each other's
    -- knowledge; if only one shelters the gain is one-sided.
    -- Status: FORM (random games, and the worked cases).

derive d-footprint-holds-cores:
    -- The footprint of a decision is what its targets rest on together with what rests on them, to any
    -- depth, and it is a union of whole cores of mutual reliance.
    -- Status: FORM (random graphs, against an independent closure).

derive d-quorum-is-cover-and-attendance:
    -- The fewest people who between them understand the footprint is the cover number; the fewest
    -- attendees such that whoever attends understands it is m - w + 1 for m people and bus factor w. The
    -- required quorum is the larger of a strict majority of the pool and that.
    -- Status: FORM (brute force over every group of people on small instances).

derive d-necessary-voices-are-sole-witnesses:
    -- The people no decision can be made without are exactly those who alone understand some part of
    -- the footprint; the affected who alone know their own stake are among them until someone else
    -- learns it.
    -- Status: FORM (brute force).

derive d-barrier-is-monotone:
    -- Raising the barrier shrinks the pool, never lowers the cover, and can make a decision unmakeable,
    -- never the reverse. The highest barrier a decision can bear is the last at which the pool still
    -- understands it.
    -- Status: FORM.

derive d-barrier-costs-capture-resistance:
    -- Each pair of seats added to a majority body makes it strictly dearer to capture, in bits, for a
    -- seat probability below one half (Condorcet's jury theorem, exact), so every seat the barrier
    -- removes is paid for in capture resistance.
    -- Status: FORM (exact rationals).

derive d-opening-closure:
    -- A permission whose consequence is a state where nothing is permitted is not coherent; a coherent
    -- opening is closed under what it enables.
    -- Status: FORM (the worked corridor, and a flag-by-flag comparison with independent checks).

derive d-no-trade-off-within-lawful-existence:
    -- Among coherent openings the greatest corrigible law is at least as good as every one of them in
    -- every coordinate, so within lawful existence there is nothing for a price to settle.
    -- Status: FORM (every law of every sampled small game).

derive d-comprehension-certifies:
    -- A coalition that understands the material of an opening can certify it exactly: the permission is
    -- in the coalition's certified law exactly when it is in the greatest law.
    -- Status: FORM (random games).

derive d-price-blind-to-the-balanced-deal:
    -- A price is a strictly positive linear weighting. An efficient option strictly inside the blend of
    -- others is chosen by no price. Shown on a menu of three doors (all x, all y, a balanced mix).
    -- Status: FORM (exact linear programs; the certificate is checked).

derive d-alternative-of-prices-and-blends:
    -- For every option exactly one holds: some strictly positive price rates it highest, or a blend of
    -- the others strictly dominates it. Both are certificates a reader can check without trusting the
    -- solver.
    -- Status: FORM (random point sets in two to four dimensions; two dimensions also against an
    -- independent interval argument).

derive d-coherence-is-not-a-currency:
    -- For any finite price on each coherence dimension there is a game in which the lawless opening
    -- outscores the lawful one: a trap of (3p - 1)//7 + 1 rooms for price p.
    -- Status: FORM (explicit family, exact).

derive d-entangled-beats-classical:
    -- In common-interest games with private inputs and no communication, entangled strategies win more
    -- often than any classical strategy: GHZ 3/4 to 1, the magic square 8/9 to 1, CHSH 3/4 to 577/676.
    -- Status: FORM (exact rationals over the Gaussian rationals; the classical value by enumerating every
    -- deterministic strategy).

derive d-value-of-a-state-is-its-degree:
    -- For the state cos t |000> + sin t |111> and the standard GHZ measurements the value is exactly
    -- 1/2 + (sin 2t)/2, so it exceeds the classical 3/4 only beyond a threshold; a product state never
    -- beats the classical value.
    -- Status: FORM.

derive d-entanglement-moves-no-information:
    -- In every strategy here each party's output distribution depends only on its own input.
    -- Status: FORM (all input profiles, exact).

derive d-entanglement-is-consumed:
    -- After the measurements the state is a product state, and a second round on it is worth no more
    -- than a classical one.
    -- Status: FORM.

derive d-no-signalling-is-not-quantum:
    -- A PR box is no-signalling and wins CHSH with certainty, which no quantum strategy does: local
    -- strategies, quantum strategies and no-signalling tables are three different sets.
    -- Status: FORM.

derive d-rival-exchange:
    -- With indivisible goods, one to a party, and strict private rankings, top trading cycles gives an
    -- outcome that is individually rational (nobody ends below where they began), Pareto efficient, in
    -- the strict core and the only allocation there, and truthful (no party gains by misreporting). The
    -- Boston mechanism, a common rule, is gameable, so the checker can fail.
    -- Status: FORM (each property from its definition, on every profile of two and three parties, all
    -- 331,776 of four by a long run outside the suite, and seeded markets of four to six; an independent
    -- reference written from the specification agrees on 9,020 comparisons in its full run).

derive d-trade-iff-inefficient:
    -- Parties trade exactly when the starting allocation is not Pareto efficient, and then someone ends
    -- strictly better off and nobody worse.
    -- Status: FORM (every profile of three parties).

derive d-audit-is-local:
    -- Each party can check the outcome from its own ranking and the public record of cycles alone: in
    -- the round it traded it received its favourite among the goods still on the market. If every party's
    -- audit passes, the record describes the top trading cycles outcome and no other. The converse holds
    -- for the honest record only.
    -- Status: FORM (every record of up to four parties on random markets).

derive d-a-common-price-cannot-see-a-swap:
    -- Two goods of equal worth at a common price are swapped, with no money, by two parties who weigh
    -- the coordinates of the goods' vectors differently. Under any common price the goods held are worth
    -- the same after as before any exchange of them, so the gain is in the private weights only.
    -- Status: FORM (exact, with the worked example and a grid of weights).

derive d-trade-iff-envy-loop:
    -- From any start, top trading cycles moves someone exactly when envy has a loop (agents each of whom
    -- ranks the next one's good above its own, the last ranking the first's above its own), which is
    -- exactly when the start is not Pareto efficient. After one exchange envy has no loop.
    -- Status: FORM (every start and every strict profile of two and three agents, seeded markets of four
    -- and five, all 331,776 profiles of four in a long run outside the suite, an independent reference).

derive d-efficient-iff-pecking-order:
    -- A start has a pecking order, an ordering of the agents in which each holds its favourite among the
    -- goods held by it and by every agent after it, exactly when it is Pareto efficient, which is exactly
    -- when envy has no loop. It is a ladder: envy points up it and never down.
    -- Status: FORM (the same markets, against every ordering of the agents).

derive d-a-wealth-start-is-frozen:
    -- The start that results when the wealthiest agent picks first, the next wealthiest second, and so on,
    -- is a ladder whose pecking order is the wealth order, with envy pointing only toward the wealthier,
    -- and top trading cycles leaves it where it is: abolishing money while keeping holdings freezes it
    -- wherever the rankings agree.
    -- Status: FORM for the equivalences (every wealth order of every profile of three agents, all of four
    -- in a long run, seeded ones up to thirty agents); FRAME for the reading of holdings made by money as
    -- a start made by letting the wealthiest pick first.

derive d-the-thaw-is-direction-not-strength:
    -- On a dial from the rankings a common price induces to private rankings (an agent scores a good by
    -- (1 - t) * price + t * its own weights), some goods and some start trade at some setting exactly when
    -- the agents' weights are neither all equal nor all positive multiples of the price: trade without
    -- money needs a difference in the direction of what agents value, not in its strength. (At the price
    -- alone, and with one coordinate, the weights are all along the price.) The setting at which anyone
    -- first moves is an exact rational, attained or not, and trade can open and close again as the dial
    -- rises.
    -- Status: FORM (exact rational arithmetic, with a witness built from the strict Cauchy-Schwarz
    -- inequality; dials derived by hand; seeded instances; an independent reference). FRAME for the dial
    -- itself: a straight mix is one path among many.


-- =============================================================================
-- SECTION V: WHAT THIS LAYER DOES NOT CONTAIN
-- =============================================================================

non-derive nd-win-win-by-construction:
    -- NOT DERIVED. That deals are win-win is a consequence of ax-freedom-is-non-rival and
    -- ax-common-interest, not a discovery about exchange. With rival freedoms or opposed interests it
    -- fails for some deals (decision D30); for indivisible goods the weak form that survives is that
    -- nobody ends below where they began (d-rival-exchange, decision D31).

non-derive nd-understanding-is-measured:
    -- NOT DERIVED. That understanding can be measured. The barrier is only as honest as the test, and
    -- whoever certifies understanding holds the gate (decision D29).

non-derive nd-institutions-are-entangled:
    -- NOT DERIVED, NOT WITNESSED. That human or institutional decisions can be entangled in the
    -- physical sense. What would count as a witness: separated devices winning a common-interest game
    -- above the classical value in a Bell-type test with the locality loophole closed, at a stated cost
    -- per use (decision D28).

non-derive nd-quantum-dilemma:
    -- NOT DERIVED, NOT MODELLED. That entanglement dissolves dilemmas between parties with opposed
    -- interests. The literature's results depend on the strategy set allowed; a deal that is not
    -- common-interest is not exclusively win-win (decision D27).

open nd-rival-freedoms:
    -- OPEN. Top trading cycles covers strict rankings over indivisible goods held one to a party. Ties in
    -- the rankings, parties who want several goods, tastes that depend on who else gets what, groups who
    -- misreport together, parties who arrive over time and a publisher of the record who might lie are
    -- not modelled, and the guarantees fail or change there (decision D31).

open nd-endowment:
    -- OPEN. Who holds which rival freedom at the start is given, not derived. The exchange leaves nobody
    -- below their start, so it cannot be fairer than the start; with identical rankings nothing moves at
    -- all. The owner has said that no uniform start is possible and that a future without money has to be
    -- extrapolated from the present world, so this layer takes the start as an input and says what can
    -- move from it (d-trade-iff-envy-loop, d-a-wealth-start-is-frozen) and not what it should be. It
    -- derives a greatest sustaining law and no starting allocation (decisions D31, D32).

open nd-ladder-dissolution:
    -- OPEN. How to unfreeze a ladder other than by a change in what agents value: making goods less
    -- rival, rotating who picks first, a transfer from the top. None is built. A transfer is not an
    -- exchange in which nobody loses, and whether it is lawful turns on what optimality is over
    -- participants (decisions D16, D32).

open nd-status-is-envy:
    -- OPEN. Real status is a taste that depends on what others hold. This layer reads standing as envy of
    -- a good and the common ranking a price induces as the dial's setting 0; tastes that depend on who
    -- else gets what are outside the model and are where the guarantees of top trading cycles fail or
    -- change (decision D32).

open nd-comprehension-instrument:
    -- OPEN. A test of understanding that a person passes exactly when they understand, that cannot be
    -- passed by memorizing it, and is run by someone with no stake in who passes (decision D29).


-- =============================================================================
-- SECTION VI: GRADUATION CRITERION
-- =============================================================================

graduation:
    -- GC-L1-0: the starting point is quoted exactly, and nothing in the file is attributed to Tyler Roost
    --          that he did not say. DISCHARGED (tests/test_spec.py).
    -- GC-L1-1: every derive is executable and checked against an oracle that shares no code with the
    --          implementation. DISCHARGED (brute force over groups of people and over every law of small
    --          games; a dense matrix oracle for the quantum engine; exact linear programs whose
    --          certificates are checked).
    -- GC-L1-2: no money and no scalar price. DISCHARGED in the sense that matters: a deal is a vector, a
    --          price is shown to be blind to the balanced deal and unable to outweigh lawfulness, and the
    --          only unit that remains is the bit (d-price-blind-to-the-balanced-deal,
    --          d-coherence-is-not-a-currency).
    -- GC-L1-3: every decision with an expectation states what would refute it, and its check can fail.
    --          DISCHARGED (tests/test_decisions.py and tests/test_clauses.py break each check, and each
    --          clause of it, on purpose).
    -- GC-L1-4: understanding can be measured. NOT DISCHARGED: nd-understanding-is-measured.
    -- GC-L1-5: institutional decisions can be entangled. NOT DISCHARGED: nd-institutions-are-entangled.
    -- GC-L1-6: rival freedoms and an exchange of them without money. DISCHARGED for indivisible goods
    --          held one to a party under strict private rankings (d-rival-exchange); NOT DISCHARGED beyond
    --          that: nd-rival-freedoms, nd-endowment.
    -- GC-L1-7: a way to extrapolate from the present start into a future without money. NOT DISCHARGED:
    --          what is shown is what is stuck (d-a-wealth-start-is-frozen) and that a change in the
    --          direction of what agents value thaws it (d-the-thaw-is-direction-not-strength);
    --          nd-ladder-dissolution, nd-status-is-envy.
    --
    -- Five of eight discharged.
    --
    -- This layer does not graduate as a theory of exchange, and no claim of one is made anywhere in this
    -- file. It graduates, if at all, as a toolkit whose finite theorems are exact and whose reach into
    -- institutions is stated as proposal.
